Debian/Ubuntu packaging notes for clipnest (P7-F)
==================================================

Source format & versioning
---------------------------
`3.0 (native)` (see `debian/source/format`) — this is one monorepo, not an
upstream-tarball-plus-Debian-delta split, so there's no `.orig.tar.gz` and no
`get-orig-source` target. `debian/changelog`'s version tracks
`ClipnestApp/project.yml`'s `MARKETING_VERSION` (the one existing version
source of truth in this repo) with no `-N` Debian revision suffix, matching
native-format convention.

Per-series builds (jammy / noble / resolute)
---------------------------------------------
A jammy-built binary would technically run on noble, but this is packaged
per-series anyway, on purpose:
- `dpkg-shlibdeps` then generates correct, versioned `${shlibs:Depends}`
  automatically for each series' actual GTK4/libadwaita/glibc versions,
  rather than one binary needing a hand-maintained union of every series'
  minimum versions.
- Launchpad builds per series for free once a source upload targets it.
- libadwaita differs sharply — verified for real: `1.1.7-0ubuntu0.22.04.1` on
  jammy vs `1.5.0-1ubuntu2` on noble (checked via `apt-cache show` against
  real `ubuntu:22.04`/`ubuntu:24.04` container images, not assumed). Target
  the jammy API from `ClipnestGTK` and runtime-gate anything noble-only.

Real bug found and fixed via container verification — `dh_dwz` vs Swift's
DWARF5 debug info
------------------------------------------------------------------------
A full `dpkg-buildpackage -d` run inside `swift:6.0-jammy` got all the way
through building and installing `clipnest` and (with throwaway fake model
files staged only inside the container, never committed) `clipnest-ocr`/
`clipnest-ocr-data`, then failed at `dh_dwz` with `dwz: ...: Unknown
debugging section .debug_str_offsets` against `debian/clipnest/usr/bin/
clipnest`, and separately `.debug_info section not present` against the
vendored (pre-stripped) `libonnxruntime*.so`. jammy's `dwz` predates
DWARF5 support; Swift 6's toolchain emits DWARF5. `dh_dwz` is a
debug-symbol size optimization for the auto-generated `-dbgsym` package
only — it has no effect on whether the shipped binary works — so
`debian/rules` overrides it as a no-op rather than trying to coerce `dwz`
into understanding a debug format it doesn't support on this series.

Real bug found and fixed via container verification — `libsqlite3-dev`
------------------------------------------------------------------------
`Package.swift`'s own comment on the `CSQLite` systemLibrary target says
"`link \"sqlite3\"` resolves against the SDK on macOS and libsqlite3-dev on
Ubuntu" — but that target has no `providers:` array (unlike `CXlib`/
`CGtk4`), so nothing surfaces this dependency automatically. A real
`dpkg-buildpackage -d` run inside `swift:6.0-jammy` (see the Known gap #1
paragraph below for why `-d` is needed there) reproduced the exact failure
this causes when omitted: `Sources/CSQLite/shim.h:1:10: error: 'sqlite3.h'
file not found`, which cascades into `could not build C module 'CSQLite'`
for every target that transitively imports it (`ClipnestSQLite`,
`ClipnestCore` tests, etc). Fixed by adding `libsqlite3-dev` to
`debian/control`'s Build-Depends.

Known gap #1 (real, verified, cross-scope) — Swift has no apt-installable
toolchain on jammy/noble
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
`debian/control` lists `swift (>= 6.0)` as a Build-Depends because it's the
technically correct constraint, but as of this packaging pass **no such apt
package exists in Ubuntu's archives for 22.04/24.04** — verified by
searching Ubuntu's real package indexes; swift.org distributes a tarball
toolchain for those series, not an apt repo (the closest, a community
`swiftlang.xyz` repo, was last updated in 2021 for Swift 5.4.2 and cannot be
trusted for Swift 6). Ubuntu 26.04 (resolute) reportedly carries an
archive-provided `swiftlang` package, unverified against a real resolute
apt index at packaging time — recheck before relying on it.

Practical effect:
- `.github/workflows/ci.yml` and `.github/workflows/release-linux.yml` both
  run inside the real `swift:6.0-jammy` / `swift:6.0-noble` Docker images
  (note: **not** `swiftlang/swift:6.0-jammy` — that image repository only
  ever published `nightly-*` tags on Docker Hub, verified directly against
  its real tag list; `swift:*` is the correct, stable Docker Official Image).
  Those images have the toolchain baked in, so GitHub Actions' `.deb`s (the
  ones attached to GitHub Releases, which `scripts/install.sh`-equivalent /
  `UpdateChecker` actually consume) build correctly today.
- **Launchpad PPA source builds on jammy/noble cannot currently satisfy
  `Build-Depends: swift` at all** — Launchpad's build farm has no network
  access and only pulls from Ubuntu's archives plus any PPA explicitly
  whitelisted as an "external dependency" in the target PPA's own settings.
  Until archive-Swift lands on a target series (or a maintained, trusted
  Swift apt repo is added as an external dependency — a decision for
  whoever administers the PPA, not made here), the PPA upload for jammy/
  noble will fail at dependency-resolution time, before a build even starts.
  This is a real constraint to solve before `dput`-ing to the PPA for real,
  not a packaging bug in this tree.

Known gap #2 (RESOLVED, kept here for the record) — OCR's hard-link problem
is fixed; `clipnest` stays a genuine Recommends
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
This gap used to be real: `Sources/COnnxRuntime/module.modulemap` declared
`link "onnxruntime"` — a HARD link (`DT_NEEDED` in the produced ELF) — so
building `clipnest` with OCR compiled in at all would have made
`libonnxruntime.so.1` a hard requirement to even start the dynamic linker,
and `dpkg-shlibdeps` would then have forced `clipnest-ocr` into `clipnest`'s
**Depends**, not just its Recommends, defeating the explicit "lighter
install via `--no-install-recommends`" requirement.

**That is no longer true — verified against the current tree, not assumed.**
`Sources/COnnxRuntime/module.modulemap` now declares no `link` directive at
all, and `Sources/COnnxRuntime/shim.h` is a fully self-contained,
hand-derived declaration of the ONNX Runtime C API structs (`OrtApi`/
`OrtApiBase`) that never includes the real `onnxruntime_c_api.h` — so
`import COnnxRuntime` always resolves, on any machine, whether or not
`clipnest-ocr` is installed. `Sources/ClipnestLinuxOCR/Runtime/
OrtLibrary.swift` resolves the real `libonnxruntime.so.1` and its
`OrtGetApiBase()` entry point at RUNTIME via `dlopen`/`dlsym` (mirroring the
`Glibc.ioctl` precedent, D59 in `.claude/project-context.md`) — never a
link-time dependency — so `ClipnestLinuxOCR`'s OCR-calling code
(`OrtSession`, `OrtEnvironment`, `OCRPipeline`) is unconditionally compiled
into the `clipnest` binary, and there is nothing ONNX-Runtime-shaped for
`dpkg-shlibdeps` to see in the produced ELF. `clipnest` genuinely stays
Recommends-only for `clipnest-ocr`, with no packaging-time compile flag
needed: `debian/rules` never passed `-DCLIPNEST_HAS_ONNXRUNTIME` (it still
doesn't, and doesn't need to — see `Package.swift`'s own comment on the
`ClipnestLinuxOCR` target), and that flag is now dead anyway — no
`#if CLIPNEST_HAS_ONNXRUNTIME` remains anywhere in `Sources/**` (see
`OrtRuntimeAvailability.swift`'s own "superseded design" note); whether OCR
actually does anything is decided at RUNTIME instead, by
`OrtRuntimeAvailability.isAvailable` (a `dlopen` check), which is `false`
until `clipnest-ocr` is installed.

The one genuinely open blocker left before `clipnest-ocr-data` can ship
real recognition is Known gap #3, below — the PP-OCRv5 model files
themselves, not anything about how `clipnest`/`clipnest-ocr` link together.

Known gap #3 — PP-OCRv5 model files are not vendored
-----------------------------------------------------
See `packaging/linux/vendor/ppocr-models/README.md`. `debian/rules` fails
the build loudly and explains why if they're absent, rather than shipping
an empty/wrong `clipnest-ocr-data`.

`clipnest-ctl`'s D-Bus action-name contract is unverified
-----------------------------------------------------------
`packaging/linux/scripts/clipnest-ctl` assumes `clipnest` registers
`org.freedesktop.Application.ActivateAction` handlers named `toggle-picker`
and `expand-snippet` — matching the same action names
`extension/src/core/keybindings.js`'s `Keybindings.ACTIONS` already use and
the two keys in `app.clipnest.Clipnest.Keybindings.gschema.xml`, but
`ClipnestGTK`/`ClipnestLinuxApp` are still placeholder targets as of this
packaging pass (verified: both contain only a single `Placeholder.swift`),
so this is a documented assumption for their authors to confirm, not a
verified contract.
